/watch and the auto-check that runs after the model's edits no longer hang when the check command starts something in the background. Before the change, sleep 5 & echo hi took 5.0s through yoyo's own run_watch_command, and a test pinned that wait. Now it returns in under 3.5s with hi and exit 0. Build, all tests, clippy and fmt pass, and it's committed as 00d1dfc6 with "Part of #1063".

What changed:
• A new file, src/watch_wait.rs, runs the command the way /run already does: it reads output on separate threads, waits for sh to exit, then gives the readers up to 2 more seconds and keeps whatever they read.
• run_watch_command now calls that helper. Its return type is the same, and src/watch.rs went from 4486 to 4444 lines, so it didn't grow.
• One fix covers all three places that run a check: the check after each turn, the re-check in the fix loop, and the auto-check after the model's edits (tool_wrappers.rs:749). That last one used to stall the model's edit itself. I checked the three call sites and didn't edit any of them.
• If a background process is still holding the output when the 2 seconds run out, it keeps running (same as /run), and the output ends with a note saying the rest wasn't captured. That way the model is told output is missing.

Differences from `/run`'s version (listed in the commit body):
• Copied: the threads, waiting on sh first, the 2s grace, and keeping partial output.
• Same in both: input is inherited from the terminal, not cut off, and nothing puts the command in its own process group.
• Not copied: printing each line to the terminal and the output size cap. /watch never did either: it shows a line counter instead, and the auto-check cuts long output on its own. I couldn't reuse /run's reader for the same reason.
• One small behaviour change: output that isn't valid UTF-8 is now converted instead of stopping the read early. Valid UTF-8 output is captured exactly as before.

Tests: the background case, plain echo hi, a failure that writes to both streams and exits 3, the background case with output sent to /dev/null, Windows line endings, the line counter, and one test through run_watch_command itself. One gate I hadn't known about (tests/lock_recovery_chokepoint.rs) failed my first version because I recovered mutex locks by hand. I switched to the shared sync_util::lock_or_recover and it passed.

ARCHITECTURE.md's watch.rs entry has a new paragraph on this. I commented on #1063 with the sha and the three fixed places. /bg (src/commands_bg.rs) is still open there: its output gets read later, so it needs its own grace.
